just about to start our parade of  Champions it's called the Irish national  ploughing championships but in reality  it's more a celebration of farming  showing just how seriously they take it  on the island of Ireland the men driving  the tractors have Rockstar status here  there's a sport here as well as a job  all these people are Irish national  heroes over three days a third of a  million visitors inspect the machinery  and the livestock it's all in the blood  and given that Ireland exports 90  percent of all the food it produces much  of it to the UK the idea of a new border  cutting the island in two is a nightmare  there's a real possibility that the  family farm would not survive a harab  races indeed potential may be this auto  farmers indeed some of the large-scale  ones could equally be vulnerable given  their level of indebtedness what the  British government wants to achieve at  this point is vague at best but it seems  to accept the huge importance of Irish  food to the UK politicians use phrases  like the All Ireland food economy which  most people don't understand  in practical terms it's much simpler so  it appears brexit has come down to this  the British government says it's happy  for meats from that cow to pass some  checks through the Irish border but not  sapele that piece of farming equipment  over there and if that appears  ridiculously picky it's also the case of  the British government doesn't seem to  realise that that would be completely  unacceptable to both the Irish  governments and the European Union the  Agriculture Minister turned up to take  questions there wasn't a single British  journalist there there's almost no  attempt to understand the Irish position  terms of the volume of trade from the  Republican ordinarily in the region of  700 million and something slightly less  coming from north salt and the granular  detail of that is you know 10% of the  the milk pool is processed from Northern  Ireland  like 400,000 sheep come sold for  slaughter I could give you a myriad of  statistics so the continuation of the  all-ireland agri-food economy is really  important to us and those politicians  who represent voters around the border  insist that if the UK can't figure out  what it wants it should let Irish people  decide for themselves this is why people  say their failure to agree on brexit  could lead to a united Ireland so the  choices are stark the British government  can either accept the backstop and the  minimal protections that it provides in  order to ensure that we don't have a  hardening of the border or they can give  the people in the north of Ireland a  choice as to which union they want to be  part of do they want to be part of the  dysfunctional so-called the United  Kingdom or do they want to as declared  in the previous referendum remain part  of the European Union the essential  contradiction of brexit remains that the  UK wants to shut itself off from the  European Union while keeping its border  with the EU open any ideas proposed by  the Irish to square that circle have  been ruled out as unacceptable in London  and yet here they're still waiting for  the British prime minister to come up  with something else instead  Lawrence Lee al-jazeera Carlow in  Ireland
 

